      "textSnapshot": "Problem: C++ headers declare their interfaces (methods, constructors, destructors) in the class body but define those members out of line in .cpp files. Because only the out-of-line definition was extracted as a symbol, classes using header-only declarations contributed no method symbols to the graph — no way to search for method names, no CONTAINS edges from class to method, and no typed-receiver resolution for method calls.\nSolution: Extract C++ in-class method declarations as bodyless method entities. These capture the interface signature and parameter types from the header, marked with bodyless: true to indicate the definition lives elsewhere. The extractor distinguishes method declarations from similarly-shaped non-members by requiring they sit directly in a field_declaration_list (the class body), and by checking that the declarator chain resolves to a function_declarator. Operator overloads are deliberately skipped because the C++ parser masks operator= and similar constructs into a uniform op stand-in, collapsing distinct operators onto one name. Function-pointer data members are correctly left as fields, not methods, by detecting parenthesized_declarator in the chain. Four test cases verify scope boundaries (in-class only, not locals or namespace-scope prototypes), constructor/destructor/override/const extraction, and C struct members staying unaffected.",
